+                data-tip="&lt;b&gt;How TASS Scores Are Calculated&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style='font-size:0.85em;color:#ccc'&gt;TaxTriage scores every detection at three taxonomic levels — Strain, Species, and Genus — using the same components but with different read sets and coverage inputs.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b style='color:#90caf9'&gt;Scoring Components (weighted sum → 0–100)&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;table style='border-collapse:collapse;font-size:0.85em;margin:4px 0'&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td style='color:#ffd580;padding-right:8px;white-space:nowrap'&gt;Breadth %&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style='color:#ccc'&gt;Fraction of the reference genome covered by reads (sigmoid-scaled). Higher coverage → stronger signal.&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td style='color:#ffd580;padding-right:8px;white-space:nowrap'&gt;Gini / Evenness&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style='color:#ccc'&gt;How evenly reads are distributed across the genome. Spiky or highly concentrated coverage lowers this component.&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td style='color:#ffd580;padding-right:8px;white-space:nowrap'&gt;MinHash&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style='color:#ccc'&gt;Signature similarity between reads and the reference after conflict-region analysis. Shared genomic regions with related organisms reduce this score.&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td style='color:#ffd580;padding-right:8px;white-space:nowrap'&gt;HMP (optional)&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style='color:#ccc'&gt;Expected abundance for this organism at the reported body site. Absent organisms score 0; typical residents score higher.&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/table&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b style='color:#90caf9'&gt;Three-Level Hierarchy&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style='font-size:0.85em'&gt;&lt;span style='background:#e3f2fd;color:#1565c0;padding:0 4px;border-radius:3px;font-weight:700'&gt;Strain&lt;/span&gt; — individual reference accession; reads that map ambiguously across highly-similar references (conflict regions) are &lt;b&gt;removed&lt;/b&gt; before scoring. This penalises the strain's breadth, Gini, and read count.&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style='background:#f3e5f5;color:#6a1b9a;padding:0 4px;border-radius:3px;font-weight:700'&gt;Species&lt;/span&gt; — aggregates all strains sharing the same species. Uses a &lt;i&gt;union covered-bp&lt;/i&gt; approach: reads ambiguous &lt;i&gt;between strains of the same species&lt;/i&gt; are re-included because they confirm the species even without pinpointing the strain.&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style='background:#e8f5e9;color:#2e7d32;padding:0 4px;border-radius:3px;font-weight:700'&gt;Genus&lt;/span&gt; — aggregates all species in the genus using the same union logic at the genus level.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b style='color:#ffd580'&gt;Why Strain 72 / Species 75.8?&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style='font-size:0.85em;color:#ccc'&gt;Example: &lt;b&gt;Ⓓ Oribacterium sinus&lt;/b&gt; Strain 72 → Species 75.8. At strain level, reads overlapping conserved genomic regions shared with sibling strains are removed, lowering breadth and Gini. At species level those same reads re-count (they confirm the species), so species coverage and TASS are higher. This is expected behaviour — a strain can score below its parent species whenever intra-species read sharing is detected.&lt;/span&gt;"
